<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arnhem</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arnhem is a city in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> on the Nederrijn, capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a>. It received town rights in 1233 and was the chief town of one of the four quarters of the Duchy of Guelders. In September 1944 it was the target of the Allied airborne Operation Market Garden, whose failure to hold the bridge there became known as a bridge too far.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arnhem.jpg" alt="Arnhem" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arnhem_Blick_von_der_Grote_Kerk_Sint_Eusebius_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Arnhem lies in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> near the German border, on the Nederrijn and the Sint-Jansbeek. It is the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a>. The earliest human traces near it are two firestones about 70,000 years ago, and the first settlement dates from about 1500 BCE at the Hoogkamp. The town did not grow up on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> but a little higher, along the Sint-Jansbeek, where the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> split towards Utrecht and Zutphen; seven streams supplied it with water, and it stood on the river itself only after the Rhine's flow was changed in 1530.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The place was first mentioned in 893 as Arneym or Arentheym. In 1233 Count Otto II of Guelders conferred city rights on the town, which had belonged to the abbey of Prüm, and fortified it. Arnhem joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1443 and was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 1473. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-guelders">Charles of Egmond</a>, duke of Guelders, took it back in 1514, and in 1543 it fell to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. As capital of the Kwartier van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluwe">Veluwe</a> it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> in 1579 during the Eighty Years' War, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English troops captured it from the Spanish in 1585 it became part of the Republic of the Seven United Provinces.</p><p>The French occupied it from 1672 to 1674 and again from 1795 to 1813. In the early 19th century the old fortifications were almost entirely taken down, and only the Sabelspoort survives of the medieval walls. The town became a genteel resort known as the Little Hague of the East, because rich former planters from the Indies settled there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In Operation Market Garden in September 1944 the British 1st Airborne Division under Major-General Roy Urquhart and the Polish 1st Independent Parachute Brigade were to secure the bridge at Arnhem. Landings began on 17 September, but the bulk of the force came down far from the bridge. Only the 2nd Parachute Battalion under Lieutenant Colonel John D. Frost reached it, and it could not secure both ends against the German 9th and 10th SS Panzer Divisions. The force at the bridge ran out of ammunition and was captured on 21 September, and the rest withdrew on 26 September.</p><p>A second battle in April 1945 liberated the city, and reconstruction lasted until 1969. The rebuilt bridge is named the John Frost Bridge, and the Arnhem Oosterbeek War Cemetery holds the graves of most of those killed in the landings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arnhem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The operation failed at <b>Arnhem</b>, ending hopes of finishing the war by Christmas 1944.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nijmegen, Roermond, <b>Arnhem</b> and Zutphen became the four quarters of the territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Duchy of Guelders</a></li></ul></section>
